# Line endings
#
# This repository is checked out on Windows (core.autocrlf=true), macOS and
# Linux, and the USB carries a `source/` copy taken from whichever machine
# assembled it. Without the rule below, a shell script checked out on Windows
# gets CRLF endings and then fails on the machine it was written for:
#
#     packaging/build_app.sh: line 30: set: pipefail
#     : invalid option name
#
# bash reads the trailing carriage return as part of the argument. The failure
# names the wrong thing - the option is spelled correctly - so it costs time to
# recognise. Shell scripts are therefore pinned to LF everywhere.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for anything else executed by a POSIX shell or interpreter
# through a shebang line, where a CRLF would corrupt the interpreter path.
*.bash text eol=lf

# Batch files are the mirror image: cmd.exe requires CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary payloads git must never touch. The weights in particular are hashed
# against eval_config.WEIGHTS_SHA256, and any transformation would break that
# check on the machine that ran it.
*.h5 binary
*.npz binary
*.edf binary
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
